  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 115 PM CST Wed Nov 18 2026\n\nA strong low-level wind field will pivot across the region \ntonight which will likely lead to sporadic 20-25 mph gusts \nprimarily across the urban corridor across northeast Illinois. \nElsewhere, the dry boundary layer will be prone to decoupling \nwith sunset, enabling modest cooling (despite the strong low-\nlevel jet ahead), and limiting gustiness. \n\nSouthwesterly breezes (gusts to 25-30 mph) will continue on \nThursday, with high temperatures getting another few degree \nboost, likely into the mid 60s for most locations across the \narea. We once again nudged wind speeds and gusts upward from the\nNBM-delivered values, given progged 950-900 mb flow remaining \nin the 25-30 kt range through daylight hours.\n\nThe surface flow will back slightly on Friday as the next area \nof elongated surface troughing develops across the Central \nPlains. Friday looks like another spectacular day (perhaps a bit\nbreezy in the afternoon), with highs pushing into the mid to\nupper 60s. The very mild start to Friday night and a fairly \nrobust low-level jet overhead will result in lows at or above \nnormal highs for the date. On Saturday, the model consensus \nhas more strongly converged on dry conditions. Breezy southerly\nwinds, sufficient sunshine prior to increased cloudiness late \nin the day, and the warm starting point are expected to support \nhighs in the mid to upper 60s again.\n\nGiven a slowing trend in the cold frontal approach Saturday\nnight, dry (and continued unseasonably mild) conditions will \nprobably extend through much of if not the entire evening for a \nmajority of the area. Rain chances will then increase overnight\ninto Sunday morning (currently peaking in the 60-70% range in\nthe gridded forecast) as a neutral tilt shortwave slides overhead\nand the attendant surface cold front pushes through. The bulk \nof the precip with this system looks to come to an end by early\nto mid Sunday afternoon, if not sooner. It will be a mild start\nto the day on Sunday, likely followed by steadily falling\ntemperatures in post cold frontal cold advection. Temperatures\nwill fall to a bit below normal for the shortened Thanksgiving\nholiday workweek, as expansive and strong high pressure settles\nover the region.\n\nCastro/Carlaw",
